1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Last time, a gene was copied into mRNA,

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:07,200
a message written in just four bases, A,

3
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:09,120
U, C and G.

4
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:11,680
But your proteins are built from twenty different

5
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:13,200
amino acids.

6
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:15,520
So how does a four-letter message spell out

7
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:17,680
a chain made of twenty kinds of building

8
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:18,560
block?

9
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:20,560
The answer is in how the message is

10
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:23,360
read, and in a small molecule that acts

11
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:24,640
as a translator.

12
00:00:25,867 --> 00:00:29,066
Suppose each base stood for one amino acid.

13
00:00:29,307 --> 00:00:31,386
Four bases could name only four.

14
00:00:31,867 --> 00:00:34,506
Pairs of bases do better: four times four

15
00:00:34,587 --> 00:00:36,746
is sixteen, still not enough.

16
00:00:37,307 --> 00:00:39,946
Groups of three give four times four times

17
00:00:40,027 --> 00:00:42,586
four, which is sixty-four, more than enough for

18
00:00:42,667 --> 00:00:43,626
twenty.

19
00:00:43,667 --> 00:00:45,786
So the code is read in threes, the

20
00:00:45,867 --> 00:00:48,506
codons you met last time, one after another

21
00:00:48,587 --> 00:00:50,106
with no gaps.

22
00:00:50,187 --> 00:00:53,386
With sixty-four codons for twenty amino acids, most

23
00:00:53,467 --> 00:00:56,026
amino acids have more than one codon, and

24
00:00:56,107 --> 00:00:57,946
three codons mean stop.

25
00:00:59,167 --> 00:01:02,286
This reading is called translation, and it happens

26
00:01:02,367 --> 00:01:04,606
at a ribosome, in the cytoplasm.

27
00:01:05,167 --> 00:01:08,446
A ribosome has two parts, a small subunit

28
00:01:08,607 --> 00:01:11,486
and a large subunit, built from proteins and

29
00:01:11,567 --> 00:01:14,926
a kind of RNA called ribosomal RNA, or

30
00:01:15,167 --> 00:01:16,406
rRNA.

31
00:01:16,847 --> 00:01:19,726
The ribosome holds the mRNA and moves along

32
00:01:19,767 --> 00:01:22,046
it one codon at a time, but it

33
00:01:22,167 --> 00:01:23,966
doesn't choose the amino acids.

34
00:01:24,287 --> 00:01:26,686
For that it needs another RNA.

35
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:32,540
That RNA is transfer RNA, or tRNA,

36
00:01:32,620 --> 00:01:35,020
a short strand of RNA folded into a

37
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:36,540
compact shape.

38
00:01:36,620 --> 00:01:39,180
At one end it carries one particular amino

39
00:01:39,260 --> 00:01:40,060
acid.

40
00:01:40,140 --> 00:01:42,380
At the other end it has three exposed

41
00:01:42,460 --> 00:01:45,980
bases, the anticodon, which pairs with a codon

42
00:01:46,060 --> 00:01:49,020
on the mRNA, using the same rules: A

43
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:51,060
with U, C with G.

44
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:55,420
So tRNA is the adaptor between two languages:

45
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:57,820
one end matches the code, and the other

46
00:01:57,980 --> 00:01:59,660
holds the amino acid.

47
00:01:59,740 --> 00:02:02,540
There are many kinds of tRNA, and enzymes

48
00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:05,020
load each kind only with its own amino

49
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:05,580
acid.

50
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:09,360
Pairing only works if the two strands run

51
00:02:09,480 --> 00:02:12,480
in opposite directions, just as in DNA.

52
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:15,280
So a codon reading G A A from

53
00:02:15,360 --> 00:02:18,640
its 5' end pairs with an anticodon reading

54
00:02:18,880 --> 00:02:21,600
C U U from its 3' end.

55
00:02:21,920 --> 00:02:25,680
Written the tRNA's own way, 5' to 3',

56
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:28,320
that anticodon is U U C.

57
00:02:28,960 --> 00:02:31,040
Keep the ends on every sequence, and you

58
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:32,320
won't confuse the two.

59
00:02:33,534 --> 00:02:36,733
Which amino acid does each codon give?

60
00:02:36,814 --> 00:02:39,373
The answer is the codon chart, worked out

61
00:02:39,454 --> 00:02:42,093
by scientists in the 1960s.

62
00:02:42,334 --> 00:02:45,853
Always look up the mRNA codon, never the

63
00:02:45,934 --> 00:02:48,133
anticodon or the DNA.

64
00:02:48,574 --> 00:02:49,933
Take G A A.

65
00:02:50,494 --> 00:02:53,413
Find the first base, G, down the side.

66
00:02:53,694 --> 00:02:56,973
Find the second base, A, across the top.

67
00:02:57,054 --> 00:03:00,093
In that box, find the third base, A.

68
00:03:00,494 --> 00:03:03,853
So G A A codes for glutamate, written

69
00:03:04,014 --> 00:03:05,053
Glu.

70
00:03:05,694 --> 00:03:08,573
And A U G, the start codon, codes

71
00:03:08,654 --> 00:03:10,973
for methionine, Met.

72
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:15,240
Now let's translate a whole message: A U

73
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:19,000
G, G A A, G C U, U

74
00:03:19,160 --> 00:03:19,960
A A.

75
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:23,480
The small subunit, already holding a tRNA that

76
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:27,080
carries methionine, grips the mRNA near its 5'

77
00:03:27,160 --> 00:03:29,880
end and slides along to the first A

78
00:03:29,960 --> 00:03:32,200
U G, the start codon.

79
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:35,880
There, the tRNA's anticodon, U A C read

80
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:38,600
from its 3' end, pairs with it.

81
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:41,320
Then the large subunit closes on top, and

82
00:03:41,360 --> 00:03:42,680
the ribosome is ready.

83
00:03:43,900 --> 00:03:46,940
The next codon, G A A, sits in

84
00:03:47,020 --> 00:03:47,980
the second slot.

85
00:03:48,540 --> 00:03:52,300
The tRNA whose anticodon pairs with it arrives,

86
00:03:52,420 --> 00:03:53,420
carrying glutamate.

87
00:03:54,060 --> 00:03:56,060
The two amino acids are now side by

88
00:03:56,220 --> 00:03:59,420
side, and the rRNA in the large subunit

89
00:03:59,660 --> 00:04:01,340
joins them with a peptide bond.

90
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:04,540
Then the ribosome moves one codon towards the

91
00:04:04,620 --> 00:04:05,500
3' end.

92
00:04:05,980 --> 00:04:09,260
The first tRNA, now empty, leaves to be

93
00:04:09,340 --> 00:04:12,780
reloaded, and the next codon, G C U,

94
00:04:12,820 --> 00:04:16,060
is exposed for a tRNA carrying alanine.

95
00:04:17,267 --> 00:04:20,306
Each round repeats the same three steps: a

96
00:04:20,507 --> 00:04:24,546
matching tRNA arrives, a peptide bond forms, and

97
00:04:24,627 --> 00:04:26,226
the ribosome moves on.

98
00:04:26,547 --> 00:04:29,186
Then the ribosome reaches U A A, a

99
00:04:29,267 --> 00:04:30,066
stop codon.

100
00:04:30,627 --> 00:04:33,106
No tRNA pairs with a stop codon.

101
00:04:33,507 --> 00:04:36,626
Instead, a release protein fits into the slot,

102
00:04:36,667 --> 00:04:38,386
the chain is let go, and the two

103
00:04:38,467 --> 00:04:39,746
subunits come apart.

104
00:04:40,307 --> 00:04:43,586
The result is Met, Glu, Ala, a chain

105
00:04:43,627 --> 00:04:46,146
of amino acids called a polypeptide.

106
00:04:47,367 --> 00:04:50,486
The order of codons on the mRNA set

107
00:04:50,567 --> 00:04:53,126
the order of amino acids, and that order

108
00:04:53,367 --> 00:04:54,326
came from the gene.

109
00:04:54,887 --> 00:04:57,886
But a polypeptide isn't a working protein yet.

110
00:04:58,087 --> 00:05:00,566
It has to fold into a particular shape,

111
00:05:00,647 --> 00:05:02,486
and some proteins are built from more than

112
00:05:02,567 --> 00:05:03,286
one chain.

113
00:05:03,847 --> 00:05:06,166
How that order decides the shape, and the

114
00:05:06,247 --> 00:05:08,406
job the protein can do, is the next

115
00:05:08,607 --> 00:05:09,046
video.

116
00:05:10,267 --> 00:05:13,226
So how important are the two RNAs?

117
00:05:13,547 --> 00:05:16,346
mRNA carries the gene's sequence out to the

118
00:05:16,427 --> 00:05:19,306
ribosome, and its codons set the order of

119
00:05:19,387 --> 00:05:20,426
the amino acids.

120
00:05:21,067 --> 00:05:24,026
tRNA is the molecule that links each codon

121
00:05:24,067 --> 00:05:26,026
to its amino acid, so it makes the

122
00:05:26,107 --> 00:05:27,186
code readable.

123
00:05:27,467 --> 00:05:29,066
Each is essential.

124
00:05:29,267 --> 00:05:32,666
Without mRNA, there's no message at the ribosome.

125
00:05:32,747 --> 00:05:36,186
Without the right tRNAs, codons can't become amino

126
00:05:36,267 --> 00:05:36,906
acids.

127
00:05:38,134 --> 00:05:39,013
Two aids.

128
00:05:39,494 --> 00:05:42,533
The stop codons are U A A, U

129
00:05:42,614 --> 00:05:45,653
A G and U G A: U Are

130
00:05:45,734 --> 00:05:48,853
Away, U Are Gone, U Go Away.

131
00:05:49,174 --> 00:05:50,533
And remember the languages.

132
00:05:51,014 --> 00:05:53,493
Transcription kept the language of bases.

133
00:05:53,894 --> 00:05:57,893
Translation changes it, from bases to amino acids,

134
00:05:57,974 --> 00:06:00,693
and tRNA is the translator.

135
00:06:01,900 --> 00:06:03,500
Two harder questions now.

136
00:06:04,060 --> 00:06:07,100
First, here's a DNA template strand from a

137
00:06:07,180 --> 00:06:09,580
gene, and part of the codon chart.

138
00:06:10,220 --> 00:06:12,220
Write the sequence of amino acids in the

139
00:06:12,300 --> 00:06:15,820
polypeptide it codes for, and explain how many

140
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:17,620
amino acids it has.

141
00:06:18,060 --> 00:06:21,100
Then write the anticodon of the tRNA that

142
00:06:21,180 --> 00:06:23,660
brings the second amino acid, with its 5'

143
00:06:23,740 --> 00:06:25,700
and 3' ends.

144
00:06:25,980 --> 00:06:27,340
Pause here to answer.

145
00:06:37,767 --> 00:06:42,886
The chart reads mRNA, so transcribe first:

146
00:06:42,967 --> 00:06:43,926
A U G,

147
00:06:44,487 --> 00:06:47,766
A A G, C C U, U G

148
00:06:47,927 --> 00:06:48,446
A.

149
00:06:48,727 --> 00:06:52,966
That's methionine, lysine, proline, then a stop codon,

150
00:06:53,087 --> 00:06:55,366
which adds nothing, so the chain has three

151
00:06:55,447 --> 00:06:56,806
amino acids.

152
00:06:56,887 --> 00:06:59,526
The second codon pairs with the anticodon U

153
00:06:59,607 --> 00:07:02,646
U C, read from its 3' end.

154
00:07:02,727 --> 00:07:05,126
Reading the DNA letters straight into the chart

155
00:07:05,207 --> 00:07:07,846
gives tyrosine first, which is wrong.

156
00:07:09,067 --> 00:07:09,546
Second.

157
00:07:09,627 --> 00:07:12,426
Streptomycin is an antibiotic.

158
00:07:12,667 --> 00:07:15,306
It binds to the small subunit of bacterial

159
00:07:15,387 --> 00:07:18,906
ribosomes, and makes them misread codons, so the

160
00:07:19,067 --> 00:07:20,666
wrong tRNAs pair.

161
00:07:21,227 --> 00:07:23,066
It doesn't bind to the ribosomes in the

162
00:07:23,227 --> 00:07:25,866
cytoplasm of human cells, which have a different

163
00:07:25,947 --> 00:07:26,346
shape.

164
00:07:26,987 --> 00:07:29,146
Explain how it can kill bacteria in a

165
00:07:29,387 --> 00:07:32,026
patient, while the patient's own cells keep making

166
00:07:32,107 --> 00:07:33,706
most of their proteins normally.

167
00:07:34,347 --> 00:07:36,266
Then suggest why it can also affect the

168
00:07:36,347 --> 00:07:38,906
ribosomes inside human mitochondria.

169
00:07:39,547 --> 00:07:40,906
Pause here to answer.

170
00:07:51,334 --> 00:07:54,693
In a bacterium, misread codons put wrong amino

171
00:07:54,774 --> 00:07:57,813
acids into its chains, so many proteins fold

172
00:07:57,894 --> 00:08:00,693
wrongly and fail, and it dies.

173
00:08:00,774 --> 00:08:04,213
The drug doesn't bind human cytoplasmic ribosomes, which

174
00:08:04,294 --> 00:08:06,533
make most of the cell's proteins.

175
00:08:06,614 --> 00:08:10,213
But mitochondria came from bacteria, so their ribosomes

176
00:08:10,294 --> 00:08:13,573
are bacteria-like, and the drug can bind them.

177
00:08:13,654 --> 00:08:16,773
If you said it changes the bacteria's DNA,

178
00:08:16,854 --> 00:08:17,813
look again.

179
00:08:17,894 --> 00:08:19,733
It's the reading that goes wrong.

180
00:08:20,934 --> 00:08:23,333
So when a question gives you a sequence,

181
00:08:23,414 --> 00:08:26,933
ask: is it DNA or mRNA?

182
00:08:27,254 --> 00:08:30,253
Turn DNA into mRNA first.

183
00:08:30,534 --> 00:08:33,493
Read the chart with mRNA codons, start at

184
00:08:33,654 --> 00:08:36,373
A U G, stop at a stop codon,

185
00:08:36,454 --> 00:08:38,773
and put the ends on every anticodon.

186
00:08:39,814 --> 00:08:42,293
Next, how the order of amino acids decides

187
00:08:42,374 --> 00:08:44,373
a protein's shape, and its job.

188
00:08:45,600 --> 00:08:47,040
Here are the key notes.

189
00:08:47,280 --> 00:08:49,200
Pause the video if you need more time

190
00:08:49,280 --> 00:08:50,080
to copy them.
